# Walkthrough - Performance & Concurrency Optimization

I have implemented and verified a series of optimizations to resolve the 1 FPS UI degradation and high CPU usage issues.

## Changes Made

### 1. Efficient Model-Level Counters
In [ScrollBenchImageModel.cpp](file:///d:/Dev/antigravity/test_scrollbench/src/ScrollBenchImageModel.cpp), I added a `m_loadedCount` counter that is updated incrementally.
- **Before**: UI components had to loop through thousands of items to count loaded thumbnails (O(N)).
- **After**: UI components can query the count in O(1) time.

### 2. Optimized Diagnostics
The [DiagnosticsMonitor.cpp](file:///d:/Dev/antigravity/test_scrollbench/src/DiagnosticsMonitor.cpp) now uses the new model counters. This significantly reduces the load on the GUI thread, especially when galleries grow to thousands of items.

### 3. Task Weighting in Adaptive I/O
In [AsyncImageProvider.cpp](file:///d:/Dev/antigravity/src/AsyncImageProvider.cpp), I introduced a "Task Weighting" system:
- **Standard Image**: 1 slot
- **Video/RAW (DNG)**: 4 slots
This prevents the system from launching too many compute-intensive decodes simultaneously, which was previously pinning the CPU.

### 4. CPU-Aware Backoff
The Adaptive I/O system now monitors **System CPU Usage**:
- If CPU > 85%, concurrency limits are cut by **50%**.
- If CPU > 70%, limits are cut by **25%**.
This ensures the GUI thread always has enough breathing room to stay responsive.

### 5. Fixed Viewport Culling
In [VisibleRangeManager.h](file:///d:/Dev/antigravity/src/VisibleRangeManager.h), I fixed a critical normalization bug:
- **Issue**: The manager was including the `image://async/` protocol in its visible set, while the provider was stripping it. This caused all viewport items to be misidentified as "offscreen".
- **Fix**: Aligned normalization logic across both components.
- **Result**: Viewport culling now correctly prioritizes the current view, and the [AsyncImageProvider](file:///d:/Dev/antigravity/src/AsyncImageProvider.h#46-47) strictly reserves "burst" capacity for visible items only.

